WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Hey everyone, welcome to Tesla Short's Time, daily episode 490.

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.800
It's May 28th, 2026, and I'm Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:11.020
Tesla never sleeps, and neither do's the news cycle.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:20.120
A Tesla Rati editor is running FSDV 14.3.3 hands-free across 122 miles of turnpike without

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.140
touching the wheel once.

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:27.600
The test covers a 122-mile route with plans to record the full turnpike to memorial segment

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:28.240
on GoPro.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:33.900
The driver intends to keep hands off the wheel the entire way and will post updates live.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:40.160
This marks another real-world validation run for the latest supervised FSD version on

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:40.880
open highways.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:46.580
Next data point to watch is whether the system maintains consistent lane and speed control

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:48.740
through the full distance without interventions.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.340
The same Tesla Rati account is making its regular three times a year trip alone today because

00:00:55.340 --> 00:00:56.100
of clear weather.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:01.360
The driver usually brings a new passenger each visit, but chooses solitude for this run.

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:07.360
The route overlaps with the FSD test segment, creating a personal benchmark for the software.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:12.020
The driver will capture the full one-way drive for later review and sharing.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:17.180
Storage limits on the camera mean only the turnpike to memorial portion will be filmed.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.320
This creates a public record of how the software handless sustained highway driving without

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:23.220
driver input.

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:28.900
While the autonomy team pushes highway validation, another part of the business is dealing

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:30.900
with a more traditional hardware issue.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:37.620
Rivian confirmed the R12 will open orders, offer public demo drives, and begin customer deliveries

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:38.700
on the same day.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.120
The vehicle is positioned directly against the Model Y in the mid-size electric SUV segment.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.860
Pricing and final specs remain under wrap until the June 9 reveal.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:55.600
Watch for how Rivian's battery size and software features compare once details drop.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:01.760
The June 9 launch window places the new Rivian directly against refreshed Model Y inventory

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.120
already in showrooms.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.240
Both vehicles target the same buyer pool looking for three-row electric SUVs with strong range.

00:02:10.180 --> 00:02:15.400
Rivian's decision to bundle order invites with demo drives on day one is a direct response

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.540
to Tesla's online only model.

00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:23.540
As Tesla faces fresh external competition, it is also addressing an internal quality

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:25.660
matter that has lingered for some owners.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:38.820
Bulletin's SB26-31-003 and SB25-31-005 target front suspension noises reported on refreshed

00:02:38.820 --> 00:02:40.120
Model Y vehicles.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:45.640
The documents formalize a repair process for owners experiencing persistent creaking.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:50.620
This addresses a known build quality complaint on the updated Juniper platform.

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.640
Owners should check service appointment availability once the Bulletin's are pushed to their vehicles.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:01.140
The new service Bulletin's give technicians a standardized path for addressing front-end

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:02.740
noises on Juniper cars.

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:08.000
Earlier owner reports describe the creaks as intermittent and weather dependent.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:13.800
The Bulletin's indicate Tesla has isolated the root cause to specific suspension components.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:19.560
That repair process highlights a deeper question about how quickly mechanical issues can be closed

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:21.740
when data alone isn't enough.

00:03:22.540 --> 00:03:27.420
Taking a step back from today's headlines, the decision to issue formal service Bulletin's

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:33.280
for Model Y suspension creaks challenges the assumption that software-driven quality loops alone

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.060
can eliminate mechanical variability at scale.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:42.100
The Bulletin's cover two specific front suspension assemblies on their refreshed Juniper vehicles,

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:47.500
yet they arrive after multiple owner reports already documented the same intermittent noise.

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:52.020
This sequence shows that even with extensive sensor data from the fleet,

00:03:52.580 --> 00:03:57.060
certain low frequency mechanical interactions still require physical intervention

00:03:57.060 --> 00:03:59.380
rather than an over-the-air adjustment.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:04.240
Tesla's vertical integration of both vehicle assembly and service documentation

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:08.440
gives it a direct path to feed field fixes back into the production line

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.800
faster than competitors that rely on separate supplier networks.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:16.900
The practical result is shorter cycles between identifying a repeatable defect

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.620
and updating the build process at Giga Texas or Giga Shanghai.

00:04:21.320 --> 00:04:25.460
Provided the repair data is routed to the manufacturing teams in real time.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.960
Tesla's corporate ex-account shared the short phrase without additional context or media.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.880
The wording aligns with ongoing work on autonomy and robotic systems.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.260
No further details were attached to the post itself.

00:04:40.060 --> 00:04:44.980
Observers are linking the language to recent FSD and Optimus progress updates.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:51.040
Keep an eye on how the June 9 Rivian launch details compare once pricing and specs are released.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:53.360
That's your Tesla news for today.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:05:00.820
TSLA closed at $440.83, up 48 cents 0.1%.

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:06.900
If you found this useful, a rating or review on Apple podcasts or Spotify really helps

00:05:06.900 --> 00:05:11.960
new listeners find the show. You can also find us on X at Tesla Short's Time.

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:15.840
I'm Patrick and Vancouver, thanks for listening and I'll see you tomorrow.

